---
import SolutionPage from "../../layouts/SolutionPage.astro";

const check = "M5 12l5 5 9-10";
---

<SolutionPage
  slug="routing"
  title="Change where CI jobs run without editing workflows"
  description="Add a relay to each GitHub Actions job once, then choose where jobs run from the dashboard: your machines, self-hosted runners, a hosted provider or GitHub-hosted."
  heading="Change where jobs run without editing your workflows."
  command="npx relayrunner init"
  close={{
    heading: "Start routing your jobs.",
    body: "Add relays once. After that, change the order in the dashboard.",
    steps: [
      { label: "In your repo, add relays to the jobs you want to route", command: "npx relayrunner init" },
      { label: "Optionally, turn this computer into a runner", command: "npx relayrunner connect" },
    ],
  }}
>
  <Fragment slot="lede">
    Add a relay to each job once. After that, you pick where jobs go from the dashboard: your
    machines, your self-hosted runners, a hosted provider or GitHub. A change applies from the next
    job.
  </Fragment>

  <div class="sol-card" slot="visual">
    <div class="sol-card-head"><b>Runner order</b><span>acme workspace</span></div>
    <div class="sol-card-body">
      <div class="sol-lane on">
        <img src="/providers/machines.svg" alt="" width="30" height="30" />
        <span><b>Your machines</b><small>3 online · 2 idle</small></span>
        <em>Next job</em>
      </div>
      <div class="sol-lane">
        <img src="/providers/github.svg" alt="" width="30" height="30" />
        <span><b>GitHub-hosted</b><small>While under 80% of included minutes</small></span>
        <em>Standby</em>
      </div>
      <div class="sol-lane moved">
        <img src="/providers/blacksmith.svg" alt="" width="30" height="30" />
        <span><b>Blacksmith</b><small>Moved up just now</small></span>
        <em>Standby</em>
      </div>
      <div class="sol-lane">
        <img src="/providers/github.svg" alt="" width="30" height="30" />
        <span><b>GitHub-hosted</b><small>No limit</small></span>
        <em>Fallback</em>
      </div>
      <p class="sol-card-note">
        <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d={check} /></svg>
        Saved. No workflow files changed.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="sol-band" aria-labelledby="how-h">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">How it works</p>
      <h2 id="how-h">Run one command, then manage routing in the dashboard.</h2>
      <ol class="sol-steps">
        <li>
          <h3>Add relays with one command</h3>
          <p>
            <code>npx relayrunner init</code> puts a relay in front of each job. The relay lists where
            that job is allowed to run.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Set your runner order</h3>
          <p>
            Each job goes to the first free runner in your order. Move a provider up or down, add a
            minutes limit, or take one out.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>See where each job ran</h3>
          <p>The Activity page shows the runner each job got and why it skipped the runners above it.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap feature feature-flip">
    <div class="sol-code" aria-label="A relay in a workflow file">
      <div class="sol-code-bar">.github/workflows/ci.yml</div>
      <pre>jobs:
  relay-test:
    uses: relayrunner/actions/.github/workflows/relay.yml@v1
    with:
      <mark>machine: self-hosted</mark>
      <mark>github: ubuntu-latest</mark>
      <mark>blacksmith: blacksmith-4vcpu-ubuntu-2404</mark>
  test:
    needs: relay-test
    runs-on: {"${{ needs.relay-test.outputs.runner }}"}</pre>
    </div>
    <div class="feature-copy">
      <p class="kicker">The relay</p>
      <h2>Each job runs on the first free runner its relay allows.</h2>
      <p class="body">
        The relay answers in about a second, needs no runner and uses no minutes. Leave a provider
        out of a job's relay and that job never runs there.
      </p>
      <a class="sol-link" href="/docs/concepts/routing">Read how routing works →</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap" aria-labelledby="rules-h">
    <p class="kicker">Rules</p>
    <h2 id="rules-h">Change routing rules in the dashboard.</h2>
    <ul class="sol-steps">
      <li>
        <h3>Fall back when your machines are busy</h3>
        <p>When every machine is busy or offline, the job moves to the next step instead of waiting.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Stay under a minutes limit</h3>
        <p>
          Use GitHub while you're under a share of your included minutes, then switch to your own
          machines.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Try a new provider</h3>
        <p>
          Move a provider up for a day, compare job times on the Performance page, and move it back
          if it's slower.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap sol-faq" aria-labelledby="faq-h">
    <h2 id="faq-h">Common questions</h2>
    <div>
      <details open>
        <summary>What if nothing in my order can take the job?</summary>
        <p>
          The relay fails right away with one line that says why each provider couldn't take it.
          Keep GitHub-hosted at the end of your order and a job always has somewhere to go.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Which providers can I route to?</summary>
        <p>
          Your own machines, the self-hosted runners your org already has, GitHub-hosted, and hosted
          providers like Blacksmith, Depot, Namespace, WarpBuild, Ubicloud and RunsOn. See
          <a href="/docs/runners/providers">all providers</a>.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Do public repos run on my machines?</summary>
        <p>
          No. Jobs from public repos skip your machines, so code from a stranger's pull request never
          runs on your computer.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Who pays the hosted providers?</summary>
        <p>
          You do, directly. A routed job runs under your own account with each provider, so you keep
          your plans, credits and discounts.
        </p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>
</SolutionPage>
